<!-- doc/src/sgml/lrg.sgml -->
 <chapter id="lrg">
  <title>Logical Replication Groups (LRG)</title>

  <indexterm zone="lrg">
   <primary>logical replication group</primary>
  </indexterm>

 <para>
  Logical Replication Group (LRG) provides an API that allows a group of nodes
  to replicate data to each other. All nodes in the group can execute
  Read-Write operatons, and any changes made on one node will "eventually"
  replicate to all other member nodes of the group.
 </para>

 <para>
  Each node in a node group of N member nodes has 1 publication and N-1
  subscriptions to the other N-1 nodes.
 </para>

 <para>
  LRG is especially useful when working with "data-aware" applications.
  It is recommended that users build a node group located across different
  geographical locations, where each node inserts or updates different tuples.
 </para>

 <para>
  Advantages of LRG are:

  <itemizedlist>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Allowing load balancing
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Allowing rolling updates of nodes
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Improving the availability of the system
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </itemizedlist>
 </para>

 <sect1 id="lrg-concepts">
  <title>LRG concepts</title>
  <sect2 id="lrg-group">
   <title>Node group</title>

   <para>
    Node group is a group that participant nodes have same tables and
    send/receive their data changes. This is done via <link linkend="logical-replication">logical replication</link>.
    Each node in a group must be connected to all other nodes. LRG make such a
    group by creating publications and subscriptions on nodes.
   </para>

  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="lrg-node">
   <title>Node</title>
   <para>
    Node is a database that belongs to a node group and it is identified
    by a user-given unique name. By definition, all nodes are not only
    publishers, but also subscribers. They must be connected to all other
    nodes in a group. If there are N nodes in a node group, each node has
    one publication and N-1 subscriptions.
   </para>

  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="lrg-configuration">
  <title>Configuration</title>
  <para>
   For a node group consisting of N member nodes, LRG will create 1 publication
   and N-1 subscriptions on each node. Therefore LRG requires several
   configuration options:
  </para>

  <itemizedlist>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     <varname>wal_level</varname> must be <literal>logical</literal>.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     <varname>max_replication_slots</varname> must be at least N.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     <varname>max_wal_senders</varname> must be at least <varname>max_replication_slots</varname>.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     <varname>max_logical_replication_workers</varname> must be more than N.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     <varname>max_worker_processes</varname> must be larger that <varname>max_logical_replication_workers</varname>.
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </itemizedlist>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="lrg-interface">
  <title>LRG SQL interface</title>
  <para>
   LRG provides several SQL functions for controlling node group.
   See <xref linkend="functions-lrg"/> for detailed documentation.
  </para>

  <sect2>
   <title>Create a node group</title>
   <para>
    Node groups are created by <function>lrg_create</function>.
    The node that executed this function automatically attaches to the created
    group. This function requires a connection string that is used for
    connecting to the node from other or connecting to itself. Therefore, it is
    not recommended to specify the Unix-domain socket or loopback address as the
    connection string unless testing purposes.
   </para>

   <para>
    When users add a node to a node group, the node name must be specified.
    This can be used for managing node information, and it must be specified
    when users want to detach a node from a node group. The name must be unique
    within a node group.
   </para>

   <para>
    All tables present in the node group must be set up to allow all changes
    to be replicated. This requires either of a primary key, or a replica
    identity with unique index and not-null constraint. LRG does not check
    them or throw any warnings. Users must define tables properly. Moreover,
    users must not change any configurations of the automatically defined
    publication.
   </para>

  </sect2>

  <sect2>
   <title>Attach a node to a node group</title>
   <para>
    In order to attach to a node group, the function <function>lrg_node_attach</function>
    can be used. At that time, in addition to specifying connection string for
    this node, a connection string for connecting to one of the node in the
    group must be specified. The specified node is called as "upstream node".
    After executing <function>lrg_node_attach</function>, this node connects
    to the upstream node, and gets connection strings for other nodes and
    synchronizes the initial data.
   </para>

   <para>
    Currently, each node can attach to only one node group, and changes to all
    tables are replicated to nodes in the group. This is implemented by
    defining a publication with target list 'FOR ALL TABLES'. This is an
    implementation restriction that might be lifted in a future release.
   </para>

  </sect2>

  <sect2>
   <title>Detach a node from a node group</title>
   <para>
    The user can detach any attached nodes by executing
    <function>lrg_node_detach</function>. This function can be called from any
    node that is a member of a node group. This function must be called from a
    node that is a member of a group.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2>
   <title>Drop a node group</title>
   <para>
    Users can remove useless node group via <function>lrg_drop</function>.
    This function can only be called from the last remaining node attached to
    the node group being dropped; it will throw ERROR if there are other
    members in the group.
   </para>
  </sect2>

 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="lrg-locking">
  <title>Locking over nodes</title>
  <para>
   This feature is under development.
  </para>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="lrg-architecture">
  <title>Architecture</title>

  <para>
   LRG implementation uses "LRG launcher" and "LRG worker" processes. The
   launcher process is registered when the server is not in the recovery mode,
   and it has a responsibility for starting LRG worker processes. The actual
   operations for creating or attaching to a node group are done by the LRG
   worker process. There can be one LRG worker per database.
  </para>

  <para>
   More specifically: when SQL functions related with LRG are called by users,
   tuples will be inserted to catalogs and backend process will notify to the
   launcher. After receiving the notification, the launcher seek <xref linkend="catalog-pg-lrg-nodes"/>
   and launches the LRG worker process. This seeking and launching will be
   also done when server boots.
  </para>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="lrg-security">
  <title>Security</title>
  <para>
   To execute some APIs, <function>lrg_create</function>, <function>lrg_node_attach</function>,
   <function>lrg_node_detach</function>, and <function>lrg_create</function>,
   the user must be a superuser.
  </para>
  <para>
   LRG launcher and LRG worker will run with the privileges of a superuser.
  </para>
  <para>
   The user used in the connection string in APIs must be a superuser. This is
   because LRG worker uses this string in order to connect to other nodes,
   and then creates a subscription in the node.
  </para>

 </sect1>


 <sect1 id="lrg-example">
  <title>Examples</title>
  <para>
   The following example demonstrates constructing a node group.
   Assume that there are three nodes, called Node1, Node2, Node3, and port
   numbers of them are 5431, 5432, and 5433. All operations will be done by
   superuser, named postgres. In the example all nodes are in the same server.
  </para>

  <sect2>
   <title>Setup</title>
   <para>
    Before executing APIs, some configurations must be adjusted. At first
    <filename>postgresql.conf</filename> must be changed as:
<programlisting>
wal_level = logical
</programlisting>
    The other parameters have default values that are sufficient for a
    basic setup. If you will test same thing in your network configuration,
    <parameter>listen_address</parameter> should be changed.
   </para>

   <para>
    <filename>pg_hba.conf</filename> must be also modified based on your
    system. In this examle all postgres nodes are in the same server, we
    don't have to care about it. 
   </para>

<programlisting>
local   all             all                                     trust
</programlisting>

  </sect2>


  <sect2>
   <title>Create a node group</title>

   <para>
    At first, a node group must be created. Here it will be created on Node1.
   </para>
<programlisting>
node1=# -- Create a node group 'testgroup', and attach to it as 'testnode1'
node1=# SELECT lrg_create('testgroup', 'FOR ALL TABLES', 'user=postgres port=5431', 'testnode1');
 lrg_create 
------------
 
(1 row)

node1=# -- Wait until operations by LRG worker are done
node1=# SELECT lrg_wait();
 lrg_wait
----------

(1 row)

</programlisting>

   <para>
    Information about the node group and nodes can checked via system catalogs:
   </para>
<programlisting>
node1=# -- pg_lrg_info, contains information about node group
node1=# SELECT * FROM pg_lrg_info ;
  oid  | groupname |    pub_type    
-------+-----------+----------------
 16389 | testgroup | FOR ALL TABLES
(1 row)

node1=# -- pg_lrg_nodes, contains information about nodes
node1=# SELECT * FROM pg_lrg_nodes ;
  oid  | groupid | dbid | status | nodename  |        nodeid        |        localconn        | upstreamconn 
-------+---------+------+--------+-----------+----------------------+-------------------------+--------------
 16390 |   16389 |    5 |      3 | testnode1 | 71141689070058577515 | user=postgres port=5431 | 
(1 row)

node1=# -- pg_lrg_pub, contains information publication
node1=# SELECT * FROM pg_lrg_pub ;
  oid  | groupid | pubid 
-------+---------+-------
 16392 |   16389 | 16391
(1 row)

node1=# -- pg_lrg_pub, contains information subscription
node1=# -- Note that no tuples will be found at the moment - subscriptions will be created when someone attaches to the node group
node1=# SELECT * FROM pg_lrg_sub ;
 oid | groupid | subid 
-----+---------+-------
(0 rows)
</programlisting>

  </sect2>

  <sect2>
   <title>Attach a node to a node group</title>

   <para>
    Next, the node Node2 can attach to the node group. Note that the upstream
    connection string to Node1 must be specified.
   </para>

<programlisting>
node2=# -- Attach to 'testgroup' as 'testnode2'
node2=# SELECT lrg_node_attach('testgroup', 'user=postgres port=5432', 'user=postgres port=5431', 'testnode2');
 lrg_node_attach 
-----------------
 
(1 row)

node2=# -- Wait until operations by LRG worker are done
node2=# SELECT lrg_wait();
 lrg_wait
----------

(1 row)
</programlisting>

   <para>
    The status of all nodes can check via pg_lrg_nodes. Following tuples will be found on both nodes.
   </para>

<programlisting>
node2=# SELECT * FROM pg_lrg_info ;
  oid  | groupname |    pub_type    
-------+-----------+----------------
 16389 | testgroup | FOR ALL TABLES
(1 row)

node2=# SELECT * FROM pg_lrg_nodes ;
  oid  | groupid | dbid | status | nodename  |        nodeid        |        localconn        |      upstreamconn       
-------+---------+------+--------+-----------+----------------------+-------------------------+-------------------------
 16391 |   16389 |    5 |      3 | testnode1 | 71141695677943317765 | user=postgres port=5431 | 
 16390 |   16389 |    5 |      3 | testnode2 | 71141695737527092585 | user=postgres port=5432 | user=postgres port=5431
(2 rows)

node2=# SELECT * FROM pg_lrg_pub ;
  oid  | groupid | pubid 
-------+---------+-------
 16393 |   16389 | 16392
(1 row)

node2=# -- A tuple will be found because now both of nodes have one subscription
node1=# SELECT * FROM pg_lrg_sub ;
  oid  | groupid | subid 
-------+---------+-------
 16395 |   16389 | 16394
(1 row)

</programlisting>

   <para>
    Now the LRG system catalogs on Node1 and Node2 have same content, so ether
    of them can be specified as an upstream node. Below, Node2 is used as the
    upstream node for attaching a new node Node3.
   </para>

<programlisting>
node3=# -- Attach to 'testgroup' as 'testnode3'
node3=# SELECT lrg_node_attach('testgroup', 'user=postgres port=5433', 'user=postgres port=5432', 'testnode3');
 lrg_node_attach 
-----------------
 
(1 row)

node3=# -- Wait until operations by LRG worker are done
node3=# SELECT lrg_wait();
 lrg_wait
----------

(1 row)
</programlisting>

   <para>
    Finally pg_lrg_info and pg_lrg_nodes will be like:
   </para>

<programlisting>
node3=# SELECT * FROM pg_lrg_info ;
  oid  | groupname |    pub_type    
-------+-----------+----------------
 16393 | testgroup | FOR ALL TABLES
(1 row)

node3=# SELECT * FROM pg_lrg_nodes ;
  oid  | groupid | dbid | status | nodename  |        nodeid        |        localconn        |      upstreamconn       
-------+---------+------+--------+-----------+----------------------+-------------------------+-------------------------
 16391 |   16389 |    5 |      3 | testnode1 | 71141714630795390835 | user=postgres port=5431 | 
 16392 |   16389 |    5 |      3 | testnode2 | 71141714693889355845 | user=postgres port=5432 | user=postgres port=5431
 16390 |   16389 |    5 |      3 | testnode3 | 71141714753722290355 | user=postgres port=5433 | user=postgres port=5432
(3 rows)

node3=# SELECT * FROM pg_lrg_pub ;
  oid  | groupid | pubid 
-------+---------+-------
 16394 |   16389 | 16393
(1 row)

node3=# -- Two tuple will be found because the # of subscriptions is N-1
node3=# SELECT * FROM pg_lrg_sub ;
  oid  | groupid | subid 
-------+---------+-------
 16396 |   16389 | 16395
 16398 |   16389 | 16397
(2 rows)

</programlisting>

   <para>
    Now each node in the group publishes their changes, and each node
    subscribes to every other node in the group.
   </para>

  </sect2>

  <sect2>
   <title>Detach a node from a node group</title>
   <para>
    Consider a case that users want to shrink its system and remove a node from
    a node group. <function>lrg_node_detach</function> can be used for that.
   </para>

<programlisting>
node3=# -- Detach Node3 from 'testgroup'. This can be done from any arbitrary member of the node group.
node3=# SELECT lrg_node_detach('testgroup', 'testnode3', false);
 lrg_node_detach 
-----------------
 
(1 row)
</programlisting>

   <para>
    The user-defined data will be not removed even if nodes are detached, but
    tuples in LRG system catalogs will be deleted.
   </para>

<programlisting>
node3=# -- On Node3, there are no tuples in pg_lrg_nodes
node3=# select * from pg_lrg_nodes;
 oid | nodeid | groupid | dbid | status | nodename | localconn | upstreamconn
-----+--------+---------+------+--------+----------+-----------+--------------
(0 rows)
</programlisting>

   <para>
    By default 
   </para>

<programlisting>
node1=# -- Detach Node1 from 'testgroup' as "force" mode - it is useful if the specified node goes down
node1=# SELECT lrg_node_detach('testgroup', 'testnode2', true);
 lrg_node_detach 
-----------------
 
(1 row)
</programlisting>

  </sect2>

  <sect2>
   <title>Drop a node group</title>
   <para>
    Suppose that user finishes operations of a system and wants to remove
    the node group. In the previous part Node2 and Node3 has been detached,
    so the testgroup can be removed via <function>lrg_drop</function>.
   </para>

<programlisting>
node1=# -- Drop 'testgroup'. Node1 will detach from it automatically.
node1=# SELECT lrg_drop('testgroup');
 lrg_drop 
----------
 
(1 row)

node1=# -- there are no tuples in pg_lrg_info
node1=# SELECT * from pg_lrg_info ;
 oid | groupname | pub_type 
-----+-----------+----------
(0 rows)
</programlisting>
  </sect2>

 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="lrg-restriction">
  <title>Restrictions</title>
  <para>
   LRG currently has the following restrictions or missing functionality.
   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Each node can attach only one node group at a time.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      All tables in the database must be replicated. That is, only
      'FOR ALL TABLES' can be specified for <parameter>publication_type</parameter>
      in <function>lrg_create</function>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      All replicated objects must be defined on the all nodes.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      LRG does not provide any mechanism for conflict handling (e.g. PK violations).
      Avoiding conflicts is the user responsibility.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>
  </para>
 </sect1>


 </chapter>
